I have been analyzing instances where the regulator's disciplinary decisions were legally challenged and subsequently overturned on appeal by the Privy Council. In synthesizing these legal precedents, I identified key cases where the court quashed the regulator's 'erasure' (striking off) rulings, labeling them as disproportionately severe. These include a landmark victory for a practitioner where the court ruled that an off-duty spontaneous dispute did not warrant career-ending sanctions, and another case where the regulator's reasoning was criticized as flawed for failing to weigh a long, unblemished career against an isolated administrative mistake. These successful appeals highlight a systemic vulnerability in how the disciplinary committee assesses misconduct and applies penalties.
